use super::*;

/// Should this record be considered at all? ROLE ONLY — an `algorithm` record; test/doc/primitive are
/// never decomposition targets.
///
/// THREE ID-SHAPED EXCLUSIONS WERE REMOVED HERE, and the reason is one rule: cohesion is a property of
/// CODE, judged by [`cohesion_reason`] from the code, and an id cannot see it. A filter on the name
/// silently guarantees a whole population is never counted, which is the failure this record existed to
/// cause rather than prevent.
///
/// - `--mod.rs` (a dir-module parent). The old reason — "a parent deliberately keeps the module's
///   stateful glue" — was true when `--mod.rs` meant "just decomposed, so its leaves are gone". After a
///   decomposition wave it means the opposite: whatever did NOT extract stays in the parent, and the id
///   filter is what guaranteed the remainder was never counted again. MEASURED: 96 `--mod.rs` records
///   hold more than one top-level fn, including `bsc-component--graph-health--mod.rs` (73 fns, 374 KB)
///   and `src-tauri--console--pty--mod.rs` (72 fns, 362 KB).
/// - `--cli.rs` / `--nouns.rs` (a command surface). Decision 3's claim is real — a match over a verb
///   enum plus the helpers that shape its arguments is ONE thing — but it is a claim about a SHAPE, and
///   the id cannot tell a record that is 130 dispatch arms from one that is 40 independent formatters.
///   `bsc-component--cli.rs` is 718 KB / 130 fns; excluding it by name means the largest records in the
///   repo are the ones nothing measures. A dispatcher's own helpers are demoted by `cohesion_reason`
///   when they are genuinely entangled, and listed when they are not — which is the answer this
///   surface is supposed to give.
///
/// This is PROPOSE-ONLY. A worklist entry is a record to READ, not a decomposition to run; the verb
/// (`bsc graph extract`) keeps its own refusals for the shapes it cannot transform.
pub(crate) fn is_candidate_record(rec: &Value) -> bool {
    rec.get("role").and_then(Value::as_str) == Some("algorithm")
}
